> Indian Railways Tatkal Waitlist with VIP Priority (Max-Heap)
  > Waitlisted passengers are promoted based on priority score 
    >  (senior citizens, women, differently-abled get higher priority)
      > Only the top-priority passenger gets the next cancelled seat.

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class TatkalWaitlist {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Max-heap: highest priority number served first
        PriorityQueue<Integer> waitlist = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

        // Priority score: 10 = senior citizen, 7 = women, 5 = general, 8 = differently-abled
        waitlist.add(5);   // general — Rohit
        waitlist.add(10);  // senior — Kamala
        waitlist.add(7);   // woman — Nisha
        waitlist.add(8);   // differently-abled — Suresh
        waitlist.add(5);   // general — Anil
        waitlist.add(10);  // senior — Mohan

        System.out.println("Total on waitlist: " + waitlist.size());
        System.out.println("Next to be confirmed: priority " + waitlist.peek());

        System.out.println("\nConfirmation order (highest priority first):");
        int seat = 1;
        while (!waitlist.isEmpty()) {
            int priority = waitlist.poll();
            System.out.println("  Seat " + seat++ + " → priority " + priority);
        }
    }
}

----------------------------------------------
o/p
Total on waitlist: 6
Next to be confirmed: priority 10

Confirmation order (highest priority first):
  Seat 1 → priority 10
  Seat 2 → priority 10
  Seat 3 → priority 8
  Seat 4 → priority 7
  Seat 5 → priority 5
  Seat 6 → priority 5

Why PriorityQueue? Duplicates (two priority-10 seniors) are allowed in PriorityQueue — unlike Sets.
And a max-heap ensures the highest-priority passenger is served first, in O(log n) per promotion.
